#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""stack — stacked-branch helper for a squash-merge PR/MR workflow.

Manages chains of branches ("stacks") where each branch's PR/MR targets the
branch below it and the forge squash-merges every one. Works on GitHub (via
`gh`) or GitLab (via `glab`), auto-detected from the origin URL. Designed to be
driven mostly by AI agents (explicit args, --json, distinct exit codes) but
pleasant for humans too (pickers, colored tree output).

- No state in the repo or in git config: a single JSON file lives in
  <git-common-dir>/stack/, shared by all worktrees of the repo.
- Local commands (list/create/split/track/navigate/restack) never touch the
  network. `submit` and `sync` talk to the forge via `gh`/`glab`.
- Every interactive prompt has a non-interactive equivalent; without a TTY,
  commands error with the exact invocation to use instead.

Subcommands (see `stack help` for the full text):
  list|ls [--all] [--json] [--no-pr]     show stack(s) + PR status
  create|c <name> [--insert]             new branch atop the current one
  split <name>:<commit> ...              split current branch at boundaries
  track [<branch>] [--parent <p>]        start tracking (chain auto-discovery)
  untrack [<branch>]                     stop tracking
  checkout|co [<branch>]                 switch to a stack branch
  prev | next | first | last             navigate the stack
  restack [--all] [--trunk] [-i] [--dry-run]  rebase current branch + ancestors onto their parents
  submit [--all] [--ready] [--dry-run]   push + create/retarget PRs for current + ancestors
  sync [--all] [--push] [--dry-run]      fetch + absorb merged PRs + restack current + ancestors
  setup                                  interactively configure options
  __complete <what>                      hidden: completion candidates

State schema (.git/stack/branches.json):
  {"version": 2, "branches": {"<branch>": {"parent": "...", "base": "<sha>"}}}
There is no explicit stack object: a "stack" is a tree derived by following
parent pointers, rooted at any branch whose parent isn't tracked (the trunk
is never tracked). `base` is the commit of `parent` this branch was last
stacked on — the lower bound for `git rebase --onto`, which is what keeps
restacks correct after a parent is squash-merged or rewritten.

GitHub's native stacked PRs are used additively, never as the source of truth:
`submit` registers the chain so reviewers get the stack navigator, and `list`
shows membership. Rebasing still runs off the local `base` above — GitHub's
headRefOid/base describe the PR, ours records what has already been replayed,
and adopting theirs would drop that lower bound. Their model is a flat ordered
list and their local state is per-worktree, so it can represent neither our
forking trees nor our worktree-shared state; ours stays authoritative.

Exit codes: 0 ok · 1 error · 3 restack stopped on a rebase conflict
"""

